<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elbe Germanic peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elbe Germans, Irminones</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> are a group of early Germanic peoples defined by archaeologists and historians in the Elbe basin, broadly matching the Irminones of Roman writers. They included Suebian peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a>, Hermunduri and Marcomanni. Migrating south and west, some of them formed the Alemanni and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elbe-germanic-peoples.jpg" alt="Elbe Germanic peoples" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by James Steakley · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urn_from_the_Lombard_grave_field_at_Putensen_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
